{0 ochre}

A syntax highlighter for OCaml using TextMate grammars and themes, inspired by {{:https://shiki.style/} Shiki}.

{image:docs/video/ochre-examples.gif}

{1 Installation}

{@bash[
opam install ochre -y
]}

{1 Quick start}

{@ocaml[
let ( let* ) = Result.bind in
let* highlighter = Ochre.load_from_files ["path/to/ocaml.tmLanguage.json"] in
let* lang = Ochre.lang highlighter "ocaml" in
let source_code = "..." in
Ok (Ochre.to_html (Ochre.highlight ~theme:Ochre.Theme.nord ~lang source_code))
]}

Loading and language lookup return [(_, Ochre.Error.t) result]; rendering never fails.

{1 Load any TextMate grammar with tm-grammars}

{{:https://github.com/davesnx/tm-grammars} tm-grammars} packages a collection of TextMate grammars for OCaml, so you can load grammars in [ochre] without managing JSON files on disk.

Install the bundled grammar package:

{v
opam install tm-grammars -y
v}

Then load a grammar from OCaml and create a highlighter. The [ocaml] grammar includes rules from [ocaml-interface], so load both:

{[
let ( let* ) = Result.bind in
let* highlighter =
  Ochre.load
    [ ("ocaml", Tm_grammars.ocaml); ("ocaml-interface", Tm_grammars.ocaml_interface) ]
in
let* lang = Ochre.lang highlighter "ocaml" in
Ok (Ochre.to_html (Ochre.highlight ~theme:Ochre.Theme.nord ~lang "let x = 42"))
]}

Try every built-in theme with several grammars in the {{:https://davesnx.github.io/ochre/playground.html} playground}.

{1 Output formats}

[ochre] can render highlighted code to several output formats (also called backends):

{ul
{- {b HTML} — Self-contained [<pre><code>] blocks with inline styles or CSS classes, as a string ([to_html]) or an html_of_jsx element ([to_jsx]). Supports light and dark themes with CSS [light-dark()], more themes via CSS custom properties, line numbers, and configurable class prefixes. See {{:https://davesnx.github.io/ochre/examples/html-preview.html} rendered example}.}
{- {b SVG} — Standalone [<svg>] elements with monospace [<text>] and per-token [<tspan>] styling. Open preview: {{:https://davesnx.github.io/ochre/examples/svg-preview.svg} SVG rendered example}. Source: {{:test/svg-preview.svg} .svg file}.}
{- {b ANSI} — 24-bit color escape sequences for terminal display.}
{- {b LaTeX} — [\textcolor] commands inside an [ochrehighlight] environment. Requires the [xcolor] and [ulem] packages. See {{:https://davesnx.github.io/ochre/examples/latex-preview.pdf} rendered PDF} (source: {{:test/latex-preview.tex} .tex file}).}}

All backends share the same interface: load a highlighter, look up a language, highlight the code with one or more themes, and call the corresponding [to_*] function.

{1 HTML}

[Ochre.to_jsx] returns a [JSX.element], so highlighted code goes straight into an {{:https://github.com/davesnx/html_of_jsx} html_of_jsx} page:

{@ocaml[
let page ~lang code =
  let themed =
    Ochre.highlight_themes ~label:"light" ~theme:Ochre.Theme.light
      ~extra_themes:[ ("dark", Ochre.Theme.dark) ]
      ~lang code
  in
  Result.map
    (fun themed ->
      JSX.render (JSX.node "article" [] [ Ochre.to_jsx themed ]))
    themed
]}

With two themes labelled [light] and [dark], colors are written as CSS [light-dark()]. Set the [color-scheme] of the page to choose one, or [:root { color-scheme: light dark; }] to follow the system setting.

The built-in transforms add the class names of the Shiki transformers. Ochre ships no CSS; style them in the page, for example:

{@css[
.ochre .line.highlighted { background-color: #8888883a; }
.ochre .line.diff.add { background-color: #22883322; }
.ochre .line.diff.remove { background-color: #88222222; }
.ochre .highlighted-word { outline: 1px solid currentColor; }
]}

